\subsection{Explainer noise is smaller than background and retraining noise}
\label{sec:floors}
Figure~\ref{fig:floors} compares the three noise floors in one metric.
Two runs of the explainer at the reference budget agree on the top-3 set for $0.91$--$0.99$ of the pairs in every non-linear cell shown; at the small budget $0.62$--$0.92$.
Two independent backgrounds of $50$ rows agree for $0.27$--$0.69$, and models retrained on a bootstrap sample for $0.14$--$0.73$ (seed only: $0.50$--$0.88$; perturbed hyperparameters: $0.14$--$0.70$).
In every non-linear cell the explainer floor at the reference budget is above the background floor and above the three retraining floors.
For logistic regression the explainer floor is $1$ while the bootstrap and hyperparameter retraining floors are $0.22$--$0.83$.
A model's top-3 set is thus much less stable under retraining, and the background under which it is explained, than under the explainer's own noise at these budgets.
Seed-only retraining is the mildest of the three definitions of floor (c) in every cell, and even it stays below the explainer floor for the non-linear models.

